--- a/specs/001-tarx-cli/contracts/cli.md
+++ b/specs/001-tarx-cli/contracts/cli.md
@@ -159,6 +159,17 @@ it where the problem concerns an operand, and the full destination path where it
 | D10 | Existing regular file would be replaced without `--overwrite` (FR-009) | 3 | `tarx: refusing to overwrite existing file '{path}' (re-run with --overwrite)` |
 | D11 | Filesystem failure after writing began (FR-022) | 2 | `tarx: failed while writing member '{name}' to '{path}': {reason}` |
 | D12 | Invalid invocation (FR-017) | 2 | `argparse`'s own usage/error output, unmodified |
+| D13 | `DEST` cannot be created for an unexpected reason — revoked permission, read-only filesystem, exhausted space (FR-022, Principle II) | 2 | `tarx: cannot create destination '{dest}': {reason}` |
+| D14 | Existing symlink, FIFO or device node where a **file** member goes (FR-023) | 2 | `tarx: '{path}' is an existing non-regular file but member '{name}' is a regular file` |
+
+> D13 and D14 were added during implementation to close two gaps in the original D1–D12 set.
+> D14 covers the `other` × `FILE` cell of
+> [data-model.md](../data-model.md#state-transitions-of-a-destination-path)'s state-transition
+> table, which that table classes as a type mismatch but which D6 (directory-blocks-file) and
+> D7 (non-directory-blocks-directory) do not word. D13 covers an `OSError` from creating `DEST`
+> itself, which D3/D4 cannot catch (they test entry *types*, and both pass when the destination
+> is merely unwritable); without it that case would surface as a traceback, which Principle II
+> forbids. Both are class 2 and both are reproduced verbatim by the test suite.
 
 **Multiple problems**: every problem found is reported, one line each, in order — destination-level
 problems (D3, D4) first, then member problems in archive order (FR-025). A member rejected by D5
